<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n115025" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">χρῡσοχο-έω</span></b> , <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>A.</b><i>follow the trade of goldsmith,</i>
			<i><span class="en">
				Ar.
				<u>Pl.</u>
				164
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				X.
				<u>Oec.</u>
				18.9
			</span></i>. </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense2"><b>II.</b>
			<i>smelt ore in order to extract gold</i> from it: hence prov. of
			those who <i>fail in any speculation,</i> as the Athenians in
			their attempts to extract gold from their silver-ores, <i><span class="en">
				Pl.
				<u>R.</u>
				450b
			</span></i>, cf. <i><span class="en">
				Din.
				<u>Fr.</u>
				6.13
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				Harp.
			</span></i></div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
    <div class="alpheios-lex-alph-source">From <u>
            <u>A Greek-English Lexicon</u>
        </u> (Henry George Liddell, Robert Scott, Oxford, Clarendon Press, 1940) </div>
